x86: park a workload thread at an instruction from the guest kernel

A fault that pauses a process with a signal stops it at a kernel entry, and
under virtual time the scheduler only ever runs at exits, so a race whose
window is user code with no system call in it is out of every pause's reach.
A hardware execution breakpoint is the missing synchronous entry into the
kernel: it traps before the instruction, needs no exit, and costs no virtual
time.

Add /dev/harmony-park, one park per open file. Arming places a per-thread
execution breakpoint on every thread of a process group as an in-kernel perf
event; the kernel counts the hits, and the thread that takes the k-th hit
queues task work for its own return to user mode and sleeps there, before
the instruction runs, until the monotonic clock passes the hold. The sleeper
is woken from the system call entry of any other task once the deadline has
passed, so the release is as fine as the other tasks' system calls; without
high-resolution timers the periodic tick is the bound. No signal is sent and
no tracer is attached, so the workload observes only time. Closing the file
disarms a park that has not fired and lets a hold in progress finish.

With CONFIG_HARMONY_PARK off the poll is an empty inline, so every kernel but
the fault-library one keeps the bytes it had. With it on, the poll moves the
kstack-offset counter read inside do_syscall_64, and the fault-library
allowlist records the new offset.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

---
--- a/drivers/misc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/misc/Kconfig
@@ -660,6 +660,18 @@
 	  The driver owns the fixed 0xe000/0xf000 frame pages and rings I/O
 	  port 0x0ca1, so applications need neither /dev/mem nor iopl(2).
 
+config HARMONY_PARK
+	bool "Harmony task park at a hardware execution breakpoint"
+	depends on HAVE_HW_BREAKPOINT
+	default n
+	help
+	  Expose /dev/harmony-park, through which the Harmony fault agent
+	  holds one thread of a workload process group at a user instruction
+	  on that instruction's k-th execution, for a length of guest time.
+	  The breakpoint is a per-thread perf event, the count is kept in the
+	  kernel, and the hold is a sleep taken before the instruction runs,
+	  so the workload sees no signal and no tracer.
+
 config HARMONY_DEVICE_KUNIT_TEST
 	bool "KUnit test for Harmony transaction serialization"
 	depends on HARMONY_DEVICE && KUNIT
